    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a1",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A1",
        "title": "Why the push ends",
        "text": "A compressed toy spring can start a cart moving. Watch the cart: after the spring returns to its usual length, an unattached cart can leave the spring behind. The cart still has motion, but the spring can no longer push it.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
          "revision": 1,
          "sha256": "20bce2ca7a6da19ef3a5051d007b1d8f5cb0480f4d25dbc5ff2577b5cab6dbd0"
        }
      ],
      "source_lineage_refs": [],
      "status": "SYNTHETIC"
    },
    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a2",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A2",
        "title": "What you need first",
        "text": "Kinetic energy is energy of motion, K=mv²/2. A compressed ideal spring stores kx²/2. Work transfers energy; a force opposing displacement does negative work. Joules are newton-metres.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
          "revision": 1,
          "sha256": "20bce2ca7a6da19ef3a5051d007b1d8f5cb0480f4d25dbc5ff2577b5cab6dbd0"
        }
      ],
      "source_lineage_refs": [],
      "status": "SYNTHETIC"
    },
    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a3",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A3",
        "title": "Make a physical model",
        "text": "Choose the cart and spring as the system during release. A contact force acts only while touching. After separation choose the cart for the next energy ledger. Transfer the release speed, not the old spring force.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
          "revision": 1,
          "sha256": "20bce2ca7a6da19ef3a5051d007b1d8f5cb0480f4d25dbc5ff2577b5cab6dbd0"
        }
      ],
      "source_lineage_refs": [],
      "status": "SYNTHETIC"
    },
    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a4",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A4",
        "title": "Why the equations work",
        "text": "For an ideal linear spring, the area under force magnitude kx versus compression from 0 to x is kx²/2. With no losses during release it equals the cart’s gain in kinetic energy. Afterwards use ΔK=Wnet for the forces actually acting. Do not assume friction acts on every segment.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
          "revision": 1,
          "sha256": "20bce2ca7a6da19ef3a5051d007b1d8f5cb0480f4d25dbc5ff2577b5cab6dbd0"
        }
      ],
      "source_lineage_refs": [],
      "status": "SYNTHETIC"
    },
    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a5",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A5",
        "title": "Read the model",
        "text": "The two labelled regions show spring contact and later motion. The natural-length marker is the boundary; diagrams are schematic. In the ideal smooth release model, x decreases to zero, stored energy falls and kinetic energy rises. Move the control and predict which quantity is unchanged.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
          "revision": 1,
          "sha256": "20bce2ca7a6da19ef3a5051d007b1d8f5cb0480f4d25dbc5ff2577b5cab6dbd0"
        }
      ],
      "source_lineage_refs": [],
      "status": "SYNTHETIC"
    },
    {
      "schema_version": "engineering-projection/1",
      "type": "PRESENTATION",
      "id": "demo:wpe:a6",
      "revision": 1,
      "subject_id": "PHYSICS",
      "payload": {
        "role": "A6",
        "title": "Build your reasoning",
        "text": "Work through the three distinct anchors: energy release, reverse design from required speed, and distinguishing an attached spring from an unattached one. Compare the underlying constraints.",
        "depth": "INTUITION_MODEL_FORMAL",
        "visibility": "TEACHING"
      },
      "dependencies": [
        {
          "type": "WARRANT",
          "id": "demo:wpe:warrant",
